in this lesson we're going to talk about  the sohcahtoa this is oftentimes  referred to as right triangle trig or  right triangle trigonometry and  basically what it is it's the ratio of  the sides in a right triangle and you  can express those ratios as the sine  cosine and tangent ratios this acronym  helps us to memorize  the ratio so basically s stands for sine  c stands for cosine and t stands for  tangent  but it's always going to be the second  letter divided by the third that's the  ratio so the sign is the opposite side  over the hypotenuse the cosine is the  adjacent side over the hypotenuse and  the tangent is the opposite opposite  side over the adjacent side so let's  look at an example here so  just a little demonstration so here in  this triangle if we want to find the  sine of angle a we position ourselves  over here  at angle a  okay  and you want to kind of just basically  look at the opposite side over the  hypotenuse so the opposite side would be  across that's seven  the hypotenuse is across from the right  angle that's 25 so the sine of a is  seven twenty-fifths  now the cosine of angle a you're  standing here again cosine is adjacent  adjacent means next to but you don't  want the hypotenuse you just want the  other side that's next to this angle so  adjacent over hypotenuse that's 24 over  25  and if you want the tangent of angle a  tangent's opposite over adjacent that's  7 over 24. now if you wanted to find the  measure of angle a like how many degrees  this angle is how how open that angle is  what you're going to want to do then is  use the inverse trig functions the  inverse sine inverse cosine inverse  tangent those are the ones that you see  above the sine cosine and tangent on  your calculator you'll have to probably  use your second key but basically here  if we wanted to find out what the  measure of angle a what we could do is  we could say well that's the sine  inverse okay of 7 25  or we could say that's the cosine  inverse  of 24 over 25 or the tangent inverse of  7 24. so whenever you want to solve for  the missing angle you're going to use  the inverse trig function if you know  the angle like in this problem here  you're just going to use a regular sine  cosine or tangent so like if we wanted  to find this side  we position ourselves at this angle over  here okay and we say the adjacent side  and the hypotenuse let's see adjacent  and hypotenuse that's cosine so what we  have is we have the cosine of 15 degrees  equals the adjacent side which is 100  over the hypotenuse which is x now what  we can do is we can just cross multiply  okay across the equal sign so that's x  times the cosine of 15  equals 100 times one and then divide  both sides by cosine of 15  and then you can do that on your  calculator and get the value of that  missing side so let's do a couple  examples see if you can pause the video  and just do them on your own and we'll  go through them together  example one says for an angle beta  in a right triangle cosine of beta is 8  17 and tangent of beta is 15  what is the value of sine of beta  okay so what i recommend on these types  of problems is to just draw a right  triangle like so here's beta  you can just think of it as angle b and  basically the cosine is adjacent  over hypotenuse and the tangent  is opposite  over adjacent so now we have all three  sides of the triangle here we want to  find the sine of beta sine is opposite  over hypotenuse so that's going to be 15  17.  another way to do this problem is if you  want to memorize the tangent  is equal to the sine over the cosine  so in this case if you know the tangent  you put that here and if you know the  cosine you can put that there and you  can solve the equation for the sine  either way you'll get the same result  okay for example two it says what is the  angle of elevation to the top of the  building so elevation is like from the  horizontal  you're elevating or you're looking up  right depression is like you're  depressed you're like looking down from  the horizontal okay  but  say there was somebody at the top of  this building like this looking down  over here at you  that would be the angle of depression  looking up that's the angle of elevation  but these angles are actually congruent  because they're alternate interior  angles because these lines are parallel  same thing with this here this height  would be the same as the height of the  building okay but to solve this problem  we want to find out what this angle is  and we need to look at the ratio of the  sides that we have we have 100 which is  the opposite side  and we have 180 which is the adjacent  side so opposite and adjacent that's the  tangent so let's go ahead and write an  equation here the tangent of theta  equals opposite over adjacent but to  solve for theta here  what we're going to do is we're going to  take the tangent inverse of both sides  you can see those are inverses and you  get the angle by itself so whenever  you're solving for that missing angle  you want to do the  inverse okay so tangent inverse of 100  over 180 you might want to check your  mode too make sure you're in degrees i  was in radians right now so just make  sure you're in the right  degrees or radians depending on the  problem so let's go ahead and do tangent  inverse of 100 divided by 180  and you can see it's coming out to about  29 degrees so that would be your  elevation right here  angle of elevation 29 degrees so the  sohcahtoa the sine cosine tangent ratios  definitely something important you want  to know and memorize for the ect
 

